#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# sequence_alignment

#% text
# `sequence_alignment(a, b)` aligns the strings (or lists) `a` and `b` globally and gives the runs they share, with a pair `[piece of a, piece of b]` where they differ (a gap is an empty piece; adjacent differences are merged). A match scores 1, a mismatch and each element of a gap -1 (the Needleman-Wunsch algorithm); among alignments with the best score, gaps come as early as possible.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
sequence_alignment("abcXabcXabc", "abcYabcYabc")
#% output
#= ["abc", ["X", "Y"], "abc", ["X", "Y"], "abc"]

#% cell
sequence_alignment("ac", "abcd")
#% output
#= ["a", ["", "b"], "c", ["", "d"]]

#% cell
sequence_alignment("One fish two fish", "One fish two fish red fish blue fish")
#% output
#= ["One fish two", ["", " fish red fish blue"], " fish"]

#% cell
sequence_alignment([1, 2, 3, 4], [1, 3, 4, 5])
#% output
#= [[1], [[2], []], [3, 4], [[], [5]]]

